0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [:db/cas 17592186246474 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186246481 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186246483 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186246484 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] [:db/cas 17592186246498 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186246500 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186246511 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & 5t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & 5t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186246999 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186246999 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+4I:$\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+4I:$\n\n$$\nA+4I=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186247000 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186247000 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-10I:$\n\n$$\nA-10I=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-10I:$\n\n$$\n(A-10I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-10I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-10I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =10$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-10I:$\n\n$$\nA-10I=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-10I:$\n\n$$\n(A-10I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-10I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-10I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =10$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186247001 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186247001 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\