}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is $\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is $\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247233 :question/problem "The matrix $S$ is given below. Let ${s}_{1}$, ${s}_{2}$,  ${s}_{3}$ and ${s}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $S.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${s}_{4}={x}_{1}{s}_{1}+{x}_{2}{s}_{2}+{x}_{3}{s}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $S$ is given below. Let ${s}_{1}$, ${s}_{2}$,  ${s}_{3}$ and ${s}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $S.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${s}_{4}={x}_{1}{s}_{1}+{x}_{2}{s}_{2}+{x}_{3}{s}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247233 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$ is $\\{{s}_{1},{s}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$ is $\\{{s}_{1},{s}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247244 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247244 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247245 :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Given the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247245 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 9 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+9{x}_{3}-4{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3} \\\\ 6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{4} \\\\ -3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 9 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+9{x}_{3}-4{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9{x}_{3} \\\\ 6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{4} \\\\ -3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247247 :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the vector $x$, if $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247247 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{{(\\,-7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,-7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247248 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186247248 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,8\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=3\\cdot {b}_{1}+8\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=3.$" "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,8\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=3\\cdot {b}_{1}+8\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186247249 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186247249 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=4\\cdot {b}_{1}+0\\cdot {b}_{2}+5\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}=4.$" "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=4\\cdot {b}_{1}+0\\cdot {b}_{2}+5\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186247250 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186247250 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+3\\cdot {b}_{2}+6\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=3.$" "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+3\\cdot {b}_{2}+6\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186247252 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186247252 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -13{x}_{2}=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(-2)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-9)+(-2)=-11.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 26\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -13{x}_{2}=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(-2)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-9)+(-2)=-11.$"] [:db/cas 17592186247253 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186247253 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186247254 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186247254 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186247255 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186247255 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom third equation we get ${x}_{3}=-1$ and substituting this value into the second equation yields ${x}_{2}=-2(-1)+3=5.$ Finally, substituting ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(5)+2(-1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-6)+5+(-1)=-2.\n$$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom third equation we get ${x}_{3}=-1$ and substituting this value into the second equation yields ${x}_{2}=-2(-1)+3=5.$ Finally, substituting ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(5)+2(-1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-6)+5+(-1)=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247256 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}