 is \"I and III only\"." "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{2}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $DE=ED={I}_{2}.$ Computing $DE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186253262 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(L)$, if\n    $L=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate $\\text{det}(L)$, if\n    $L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186253266 :question/problem "If\n    $M=[\\begin{aligned}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$\\text{det}(M)=2$, what is the value of $w?$" "If\n    $M=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(M)=2$, what is the value of $w?$"] [:db/cas 17592186253311 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253312 :question/problem "If $F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & t \\\\ t & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$" "If $F=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & t \\\\ t & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186253313 :question/problem "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ x & x\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$" "If $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ x & x\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186253320 :question/problem "If $P=[\\begin{aligned}n & -8 \\\\ -2 & n\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$" "If $P=\\begin{bmatrix}n & -8 \\\\ -2 & n\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186253579 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253579 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+3\\cdot y \\\\ 0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot x+3\\cdot y \\\\ 0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253584 :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186253584 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=2.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $2.$" "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=2.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186253586 :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186253588 :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]]
